.TH DANBYLAB 2
.SH NAME
danbylab: run, parse, masses, equilibrium, march \- Danby models as scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "draw.m";
include "danbylab.m";
danbylab := load Danbylab Danbylab->PATH;

err := danbylab->run(ctxt, name, spec);
(m, cfg, err) := danbylab->parse(spec);

mass := danbylab->masses(m, cfg);
(x, y) := danbylab->equilibrium(m, cfg);
data := danbylab->march(m, cfg);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Danbylab
is the whole of the Danby viewer \(em window, integrator, history and
controls \(em as a library the model calls with its own script:
.PP
.EX
danbylab->run(ctxt, "threebody", SPEC);
.EE
.PP
Two languages share that script.
.IR Dynsys (2)
reads the equations:
.BR param ,
.BR maxtime ,
.BR state ,
.BR derivative ,
.BR eq ,
.B observable
and
.BR point .
.I Danbylab
reads everything that says what the picture is, and returns it as a
.BR Config .
The split is the design: the model is the equations, the config is the
picture, and
.B parse
returns both and opens no window, so a test can check a model's numbers
where there is no display at all.
.SS The shared vocabulary
.TP
.BI family " name"
Which of the seven families this model belongs to \(em
.BR mechanism ,
.BR population ,
.BR trajectory ,
.BR orbit ,
.BR ecology ,
.B flight
or
.BR profile .
Required, and required first: it chooses the panels, the graph and the
rules a step obeys, and a family's own words are refused in any other.
.TP
.BI title " text..."
The window title.  Required.
.TP
.BI manual " page"
The manual page the window manager's Help menu opens.  A model that
names none is assumed to have a page called
.BI danby- name \fR.
.SS The families
.B Mechanism
draws a linkage beside a two-trace graph.  It needs
.B point
lines, exactly two
.B observable
lines, and at least one
.BR link :
.TP
.BI link " name name"
A rod between two of the model's points.  Repeatable.
.PP
.B Population
draws a stack of bars beside a graph with one trace per state; a click in
a bar sets that state.  It has no words of its own.
.PP
.B Trajectory
is a flight in a vertical plane: four states, a position and its
velocity, and a
.B maxtime
the run ends at, or sooner if the height crosses zero.
.PP
.B Orbit
draws bodies and their trails on a night sky, the most massive one
pinned at the centre of the view.  Four states per body \(em
.IR x ,
.IR y ,
then the two velocity components \(em and exactly two
.B observable
lines, which are the two traces:
.TP
.BI body " name expr"
A body, its label on the diagram, and its mass.  Repeatable.  The mass
is an EXPRESSION over the parameters, not a number, because in this
family a slider usually IS a mass; it is evaluated at every reset.
.PP
.B Ecology
draws a phase portrait beside a time series for exactly two species; a
click in the portrait starts again at the point clicked, and a slider
bends the trajectory that is already running rather than restarting it:
.TP
.BI equilibrium " xexpr " , " yexpr"
Where the two rates vanish, as expressions over the parameters.
Required \(em it is what this family shows.  The comma is a word of its
own, because either half may have spaces in it.
.TP
.BI scale " num"
Pin both phase axes at
.IR num .
Without this they grow to hold the trajectory.
.PP
.B Flight
is a flight in three dimensions: six states \(em downrange, lateral and
height, then their three velocities \(em drawn as a side view above a
plan view.  Unlike the flat families an aim or a crosswind is signed, so
a negative parameter minimum is meaningful.
.PP
.B Profile
is a still picture: a march outward in radius rather than forward in
time, with no clock and no Pause button.  Exactly two
.B observable
lines, which are the two curves:
.TP
.BI radius " startexpr " , " spanexpr"
Where the march starts and how far it runs.  Required.
.TP
.BI samples " n"
How many steps the march takes.  At least two; 320 by default.
.TP
.BI normalise " name"
Divide the named observable by its own outermost value, so a curve that
is an absolute quantity can share an axis with one that is a fraction.
.SS Evaluating what the spec declared
The three families whose extra language is arithmetic evaluate it
through
.IR danbylab ,
so a test can ask for the same numbers the picture shows.
.B Masses
returns the
.B body
masses,
.B equilibrium
the
.B equilibrium
point, and
.B march
the profile as
.IR samples
triples of radius and the two observables.  Each needs the model's
parameters already bound, which
.B dynsys->initialstate()
and
.B dynsys->rhs()
both do, so call one of those first.
.SS The pictures
The moving parts are sprites, not circles.
A mechanism's masses are drawn as a weight and the points it is bolted to
as an anchor; an orbit's bodies as a sun, a planet or a moon by what they
weigh; and a trajectory or a flight has the thing that is flying drawn at
the end of its path, turned to face the way it is going.
.PP
They are
.IR vsprite (2)
sprites, which is to say element lists in
.BR /lib/danby/sprites ,
so each is drawn at whatever size the pane calls for and at whatever
angle the model is pointing rather than at one size somebody rasterised
it to.
They are the same text form
.IR temple / spriteedit (1)
edits, so the picture a model draws can be changed without touching any
Limbo.
.PP
A spec says
.BI "sprite " name
to choose one.
Saying nothing gets the family's default, which is why the 151 models
written before there were any needed no editing;
.B ball
is the default for the two families that fly, and the five that launch a
rocket rather than hit a ball say so.
.PP
Which points of a mechanism are fixed is decided by watching rather than
by anything in the spec: a point that has never left where it started is
what the thing is bolted to.
No spec says which point is a pivot, and a hoop's centre and a pendulum's
pivot are both simply points that do not move.
.PP
A model whose sprite file is missing draws the circles it always drew, so
.B /lib/danby/sprites
is not a dependency.
.SS "Seeing that a sprite reached the pixels"
Create
.B /env/DANBY_PIXELS
and the library reads its own diagram pane back at the sixtieth frame and
prints how many distinct colours are in it and how many pixels each
holds.
This is in-process evidence and depends on nothing outside
.IR emu :
a screenshot cannot say whether the ellipse under a sprite was drawn, and
reading the code cannot either.
.EX
; bind -a '#e' /env
; echo -n > /env/DANBY_PIXELS
; danby/simplependulum
danby census The simple pendulum: 7 colours
	f4f0e7 350654
	b0b4ba 366
	...
	c02428 138
	ff6b60 39
.EE
The colours are the RGB that
.IR grpalette (2)
names.
A model drawing circles has three or four; one drawing a sprite has seven
or more, and the counts say which sprite: 138 pixels of palette 4 with 39
of palette 12 on top is the bob, and
.I doublependulum
shows exactly twice that.
An absolute count is the pose at that frame and no more \(em a body a few
degrees further round rasterises to a pixel or two either way \(em so the
comparisons worth making are the ones that do not depend on the pose, of
which the doubling is one.
Move
.B /lib/danby/sprites
away and the same census falls back to three colours, which is the
control.
Give the file a number to census a different frame; an empty file means
the sixtieth.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/danbylab.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danbylabtest (1),
.IR dynsys (2),
.IR vsprite (2),
.IR plot (2),
.IR panel (9)
.SH BUGS
A window is a window: everything from
.B run
onward needs
.IR draw (2)
and
.IR tk (2),
and only
.B parse
and the three evaluators run without them.
.PP
A sprite is turned but never flipped, so a trajectory that comes back the
way it went shows the flying thing nose-first in both directions.
.PP
The size a mechanism's parts are drawn at follows the pane's width and
not the mechanism's own scale, so a model whose parts are very far apart
gets parts that look large for it.
